The desk merged **412 reports** into #4471 and marked it known as one issue: the parser
reads dates month first. The export shows two causes, not one. They share a symptom —
a booking lands on the wrong day — and nothing else.

## The two causes

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

- **parser** — the shared parser reads every date month first. All 11 tenants, any channel.
  This is the known issue; the fix is queued.
- **hint** — the `booking-v3` form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, but the form
  reads the date the tenant's way: day first. Only Alder Physio, Copper Studio and Pale
  Harbour (all on `booking-v3`), only reports sent through the form. A different bug with
  no ticket of its own: it was merged into #4471 by subject.

## Why they were merged

Dedupe matches subject lines ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
"Wrong day", "invite is wrong", "dates mixed up" — both bugs produce those subjects.
Fast during an incident, and blind to cause: reading the bodies is what separates them.

## What the export adds up to

The export has 427 rows and 412 reports: the ticket system pages overlap and 15 ids come
twice. Counted once, the 412 split exactly into the two causes above. Intake's guess
(`intakeTag`) disagrees with the cause on 38 reports — the table's *tagged as* column shows
both guesses; the cause comes from the tenant's locale and form, not the reporter's browser.
Five reports the desk read as a one-day move are filed under their cause by tenant.

## Lessons

- A big ticket is mostly one bug reported many times — but not always only one bug.
- A count per ticket is honest only when every cause in the ticket has its own count.

